From ccd055f186c276c5001f3d6681612d8338a9de7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 10:50:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] Add TrimIdleThreads: idle threads give back their block
 caches and old code buffers (default off)

A thread only moves to a new code buffer when it next compiles, so threads that
sit in waits keep every buffer they used alive, along with a dynamic L1 of up to
16 MB each. On a phone this was about 112 MB of old code buffers and 110 MB of L1,
nearly all of it in swap.

With TrimIdleThreads=N, a FEX-internal thread checks every N seconds for threads
that used no CPU time since the last check. Each is suspended exactly as
ThreadTerm does (NtSuspendThread then NtGetContextThread, so it is outside the
JIT and holds no JIT locks), its L1, L2, page map and call-return stack are
decommitted, and it is pointed at the newest code buffer. Nothing is allocated or
freed while it is suspended; old buffers are dropped after it resumes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

The worker starts with the process's second thread, never during process init,
and its first pass waits at least 30 s. Threads are opened, timed and resumed with ntdll
calls only, so the emulator DLL imports nothing but ntdll.
